<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solomonic dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from <a href="/years/1270">1270</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Solomonic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Solomonic dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> from 1270, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a>, until the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> in 1974. It claimed descent from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> describe this lineage as a basis of royal legitimacy alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty.jpg" alt="Solomonic dynasty" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_coat_of_arms_of_Ethiopia_(Haile_Selassie).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Modern historians give the name Solomonic dynasty to the line of Ethiopian Orthodox Christian monarchs founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a>, a noble from the Kingdom of Shewa, who overthrew the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe</a> ruler and in 1270 established his capital at Bete Amhara. Ethiopian tradition holds that he descended in the male line from the royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, and through it from Menelik I, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>. The canonical form of the claim appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a>, a 14th-century text, which says Yekuno Amlak restored the Solomonic line.</p><p>The historian Harold G. Marcus calls its stories a pastiche of legends created to legitimise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a>'s seizure of power, and David Northrup judges the descent highly improbable and unsupported by evidence. Even so, the story became embedded in the Ethiopian sense of nationhood, and the 1955 constitution declared that the emperor descends without interruption from Menelik I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The effective realm of the dynasty was long the central and northern highlands, with capitals that shifted. Zera Yaqob I established the first capital city at Debre Birhan on 7 March 1456, Fasilides moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> in 1636, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> moved it to Addis Abeba after 1889. The Gondarine line began with Susenyos I in 1606 and ended with the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a> in 1889. Menelik II, king of Shewa from 1866, became emperor in 1889 and reigned until 1913.</p><p>His daughter Zewditu was the last monarch able to claim uninterrupted male descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and Sheba, since Lij Iyasu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> descended through the female line. The southern and eastern regions of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> were permanently incorporated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> and Haile Selassie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The dynasty ruled until 1974, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> in the Ethiopian Revolution. Members of the imperial family then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> were imprisoned, and some were executed. The women were released in 1989 and the men in 1990, and the rest left the country in 1991, when the communist government fell. The Lion of the Tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, which stood at the centre of the imperial flag, is also a leading religious symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari movement</a>, which regards Haile Selassie as divine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solomonic dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Solomonic dynasty</b> was established in 1270, traditionally on 10 August, when Yekuno Amlak overthrew the last Zagwe king at the Battle of Ansata.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It purports to trace the origins of the <b>Solomonic dynasty</b>, the line of Ethiopian Orthodox Christian monarchs who ruled the country until 1974, to the biblical king Solomon and the Queen of Sheba through Menelik I. Modern scholarship considers its stories to have been largely created to legitimise…</blockquote><a href="/work/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
